  "summary": "Fourteen published items; none cardiac. Fibrosis appears only in two pulmonary studies (luteolin, biodiesel formaldehyde).",
  "synthesis": "The window contains 14 published items and no preprints, code, or industry signals, with a 3.4 rise ratio and zero 7-day star gain. None addresses cardiac fibrosis directly. The fibrosis-relevant content is limited to two pulmonary studies: luteolin attenuating bleomycin-induced pulmonary fibrosis via reduced AKT, mTOR, HIF-1α and MMP2 in mice, and formaldehyde from biodiesel exhaust identified as driving pulmonary fibrosis through EMT and TGF-β1. The remaining items are largely network toxicology and multi-omics pipelines applying WGCNA, machine learning, Mendelian randomization and docking to environmental exposures such as phthalates, lindane, triclosan, and microplastics, across lung, prostate, breast, thyroid and bone cancers. Their computational predictions are hypothesis-generating. The observed rise reflects publication volume alone; the items do not establish a cardiac fibrosis mechanism or a cause for the increase.",
